    "content": "CARE International has issued a serious alert about the humanitarian situation in Somalia, noting that reduced relief funding has led to the closure of numerous health and nutrition facilities across the country. The aid organization says this development poses a direct threat to young children, mothers, and pregnant women living in the most vulnerable regions.\n\nWhile speaking on the matter, CARE's Country Director for Somalia, Ummy Dubow, explained that four consecutive failed rainy seasons have already pushed millions of people into critical conditions. She added that recent flooding and the effects of the El Niño climate pattern have further aggravated the suffering of families who are already struggling to meet basic needs.\n\nThe closure of these centers is particularly concerning because they provide essential services such as maternal care, immunizations, and therapeutic feeding for malnourished children. Without these facilities, many households in remote and conflict-affected areas have little or no access to life-saving treatment, increasing the risk of preventable deaths.\n\nThis situation matters deeply for the Somali diaspora, many of whom regularly send remittances and support relief efforts back home. The warnings from CARE underscore the urgent need for sustained international funding and diaspora engagement to prevent the crisis from escalating further. Health and nutrition programs remain critical lifelines for communities that have endured years of instability and climate shocks.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/30/care-oo-ka-digtay-khatarta-macaluul-iyo-xiritaanka-xarumo-caafimaad-oo-dalka-ku-yaalla/",
