    "content": "A senator representing Puntland in Somalia's Upper House of the Federal Parliament has staged a one-man protest inside the chamber, insisting that his term has ended and that the government is operating without legal authority. The senator, who has not been named in official records, argued that the federal government no longer has the mandate to enact new legislation and should limit its activities to consulting on election preparations.\n\nThe protest comes at a time of heightened political friction between the federal government in Mogadishu and the semi-autonomous Puntland administration. Puntland has repeatedly clashed with the federal government over issues such as resource sharing, electoral reforms, and the extension of mandates, which have strained relations in recent months. The senator's action reflects broader discontent within Puntland's political circles, who accuse the federal government of overstepping its bounds.\n\nSenator Qaybdiid, a former member of the security forces, was seen as the key figure behind the protest, though official sources have not confirmed his involvement. The protest has drawn attention to the growing crisis of legitimacy in Somalia's political institutions, which have been plagued by delays and disputes over the electoral timetable.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this political instability underscores the challenges facing the country's progress toward democratic governance. The diaspora, which plays a significant role in Somalia's economy and political discourse, will be watching closely to see if the federal government heeds calls to prioritize elections and resolve the impasse with Puntland peacefully.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/29/somali-senator-calls-for-elections/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=somali-senator-calls-for-elections",
